course: dictionary
lesson: 14701
title: Principally
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Principally» is a formal word for «mainly». It tells you which part is the biggest or most important. «The club is principally for beginners.» This means most of the club is for beginners, though others may come too.
explain: «The bag is principally made of cotton, with a little plastic on the strap.» The words «with a little» give the clue: cotton is the biggest part, but plastic is there as well. Principally does not mean only.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in «mainly». If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right. «The bag is mainly made of cotton» works, so principally means mainly here.
fact: Principally means mainly, mostly, or more than anything else.
fact: «Principally» is a formal word, often found in reports, science texts and exam passages.
fact: «Principally» does not mean only. Other parts can still be there.
fact: Useful phrases: «principally because», «principally made of», «principally used for».
fact: «The class is principally girls» means more than half the class are girls.
exercise: «The park is principally used for football.» What does principally mean here? | Not at all | *Mainly | Very early | Quietly
exercise: «The soup is principally made of carrots and lentils.» What does principally suggest? | There is no soup at all | *Carrots and lentils are the biggest part | The soup is cold | Carrots and lentils are the smallest part
exercise: Which word means the same as principally? | *Mainly | Sadly | Rarely | Quietly
test: «The garden is principally full of roses, with a few tulips.» What does principally tell us? | There are no roses at all | *Roses are the biggest part of the garden | Tulips are the biggest part of the garden | The garden is very small
test: «The rain fell principally in the north, and the south stayed dry.» Where did most of the rain fall? | Everywhere equally | Nowhere at all | In the south | *In the north
exam: «The museum is principally for school groups, though anyone can visit.» Which meaning fits best? | *Mainly for school groups, and others can visit too | Free for everyone and always empty | Closed on Sundays | Only for school groups, and nobody else may enter
